  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front and associated low pressure will \nlift across the area today creating unsettled conditions.\n\nA warm front will continue lifting north through the region this \nmorning. A weak area of low pressure attached to the front will\nalso add some more lift to the synoptic situation this afternoon.\nIncreasing southerly flow thru today will push dew points up into\nthe low to mid 70s this afternoon. With highs in the low to mid\n80s, max heat index values will be in the upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nIn terms of precipitation, some showers will develop in the morning\nwith the passage of the warm front, and then another round of showers\nand scattered thunderstorms will develop in the afternoon. The primary\nconcern is for heavy rain leading to flash flooding, but when it\ncomes to a hot and humid airmass, a few strong to severe thunderstorms\ncannot be ruled out. \n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk (1 out of 4) for\nExcessive Rain for today. Rainfall amounts will be light in the morning.\nWhile overall basin averages will generally be 1/4 to 1/2 inch of\nrain, there is the potential for training leading to localized rainfall\ntotals in excess of 1 inch. With most of that rain occurring in the\nafternoon, that creates the potential for flash flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...For the later half of the week, we will have a \nhot, humid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nExpect a prolonged period of southwesterly flow as our region will\nbe in between a Bermuda High well to our east, and a mid-level\nlong wave trough that will be slowly sliding east over the northern\ntier of the U.S./southern Canada. As such, it will be hot and humid\nfor the later half of the week and weekend with maximum temperatures\neach day ranging from the low 80s (Poconos) to the low 90s (metro).\nHowever, with dewpoints from 70F to 75F, heat index values at or\nabove 100 degrees are possible for much of the area. \n\nThe other aspect of this are the daily storm chances, primarily\nconfined to the afternoon and evening with a typical diurnal pattern.\nGiven the hot and humid conditions that will be in place, we can't\nrule out the more common thunderstorm hazards of damaging winds\nand heavy rain/flooding. If an embedded short wave trough crosses\nour region as the larger trough axis approaches, that could increase\nthe risk, particularly for severe threats, but trying to time that\nout at this point would be very difficult, given that we are still\nseeing some differences between the models with the larger pattern.",
